source is UNSUPPORTED\". The claim \"above GISMo's 20.56\" is a factual claim without a source → UNSUPPORTED. But actually maybe the whole item is a prediction (\"Hit@1 rises with width\") which needs no source, and the 20.56 reference is context. This is ambiguous. I think the stricter reading: item has empty sources, so any factual claim in it is unsupported. But the prediction itself needs no source. PARTIAL? The rubric statuses: SUPPORTED, PARTIAL, UNSUPPORTED, CONTRADICTED, UNREACHABLE. For cards with no sources and pure predictions, SUPPORTED with note \"prediction needs no source\". If they contain an unverifiable factual claim, maybe PARTIAL/UNSUPPORTED.\n\nLet me think about consistency: items like card:test_summary contain \"20.56 baseline\" and \"Reproduce GISMo's Recipe1MSubs result\" — factual claims? \"Reproduce ... result... if Hit@1 climbs past the 20.56 baseline\" — the 20.56 baseline is a factual claim about GISMo's published result. No source in the item. Hmm. But these are plan fields describing what the plan will do. The rule says \"what the plan will build, measure or predict needs no source\". The test_summary describes what the plan will do — training at widths, five seeds, four GPUs — that's the plan's action, no source needed. The \"20.56 baseline\" embedded is a factual claim though.\n\nI think a reasonable approach: for these cards, the embedded references to 20.56 are plan-internal (the plan adopts the baseline; the anchor card cites the source for it). The judge's instruction: \"check each factual claim in it against those sources\" — \"those sources\" refers to the sources the item cites. If the item cites none, factual claims are UNSUPPORTED. But then card:if_not would be UNSUPPORTED.\n\nHmm, but maybe more sensible: items that are pure plan content with no cited factual claims → SUPPORTED. Items referencing external facts without sources → UNSUPPORTED (or PARTIAL if mixed).\n\nLet me be careful and consistent: I'll treat embedded bas